#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175690 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1034760-066d-5481-a644-ea198add71ec" :knowledge-point/title "One-to-One and Many-to-One Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "cd699f35-88bd-57b5-8413-f26ee0752f18" :question/math-academy-id "e-10130" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/5e/5e5fdd7655bb0cb4858b2a723f3eb3a7bf16c290b98dde097153c0bc78c4ac54.svg)\nIf the probability density function of $Y = X^2$ is given by \n![](images/d4/d436eaaafafcc03b81ac40cd746dcdf7728055cab8ea6c3ac1f3cb36279fd219.svg)\nfind the function $h(y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's visualize the transformation $Y = X^2$ over the interval $-1 <            x <         2.$\n\n![](images/c9/c98b2d947486e4666380dc8412c1b64a8518c84e39372052602a9402ab3d4211.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $-1 \\lt   x <       1.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $0 \\lt  y \\lt 1.$\n\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $1 \\leq x \\lt  2.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $1 \\leq  y \\lt   4.$\n\nIn this question, we're asked to consider only the interval $1 \\leq  y \\lt   4.$\n\nUsing the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $1 \\leq  y \\lt   4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P({X}^{2}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\sqrt{{X}^{2}}\\le \\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =P(|X|\\le \\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(\\sqrt{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used $|X| = X$ for $X > 0.$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $1 \\leq  y \\lt   4,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\sqrt{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{33}(\\sqrt{y})^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{33}{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{66}\\,{y}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $h(y) = \\dfrac{5}{66}\\,y^{3/2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175850 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176072 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f695215-606d-5c8b-87d2-dcf08bdc84e3" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Two-to-One Transformations to Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "501ad713-9e9d-53d7-ac8e-397edd64bee1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9161" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/a7/a7c708ba41cf7369d6d99e43389c1a4d52e92c77d7051ced888fd57c111c82a9.svg)\nIf $Y = |X|,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the transformation $Y = |X|$ is two-to-one over the interval $-3\\lt x \\lt 3.$ \n\n![](images/8b/8bc657a6b1b0defb0857f665ae48353862f06e0145bbc22ad3dac906da720380.png)\n\nMoreover, for $-3\\lt x \\lt 3,$ we have that $0 \\leq y \\lt 3.$ \n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0 \\leq      y <           3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(|X|\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-y\\le X\\le y) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(y)-{F}_{X}(-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $0 \\leq      y <           3,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}^{′}(y)-(-1){F}_{X}^{′}(-y) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}^{′}(y)+{F}_{X}^{′}(-y) \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(y)+{f}_{X}(-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}(y)+{f}_{X}(-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(y+1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}(-y+1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}[({y}^{2}+2y+1)+({y}^{2}-2y+1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(2{y}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}({y}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n![](images/73/732a2d8d53f1424b086596203b917e51f1e791bdaef7b638eb109f3ee5600219.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176072 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f695215-606d-5c8b-87d2-dcf08bdc84e3" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Two-to-One Transformations to Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "211ad08f-eb3c-5473-9d00-fea49fea3f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{8}, & 0\\le y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551737} {:answer/id #uuid "9731e6ad-09ad-5aa3-9bd6-67f4eba3dfca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{y}}, & 0\\le y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552029} {:answer/id #uuid "aa683d30-4f48-5983-b071-9e6b53636eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}, & 0\\le y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552074} {:answer/id #uuid "c66bed31-c011-512b-a78c-c27878e6d104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{y}}{16}, & 0\\le y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552137} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3cac1a-7df3-5a5c-8cb5-6c70639ca2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2}, & 0\\le y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9731e6ad-09ad-5aa3-9bd6-67f4eba3dfca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{y}}, & 0\\le y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552029} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8b4d77c-02ba-56ca-a4f9-ffe483ffd99e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be91f77e-a6ef-5326-b655-78aac371668c" :question/math-academy-id "q-144031" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}, & -1<x<3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y={(1-X)}^{2},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the transformation $Y={(1-X)}^{2}$ is two-to-one over the entire interval $-1<x<3.$ \n\n![](images/03/03718360bf910c74c22b36f34ad2f63bfad6d9387c0cdc62d3fb366a52f356c9.png)\n\nMoreover, for $-1<x<3,$ we have that $Y$ is nonzero on $0\\le y<4.$ \n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0\\le y<4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P({(1-X)}^{2}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-{y}^{1/2}\\le 1-X\\le {y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =P(-{y}^{1/2}-1\\le -X\\le {y}^{1/2}-1) \\\\  & =P(1-{y}^{1/2}\\le X\\le 1+{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(1+{y}^{1/2})-{F}_{X}(1-{y}^{1/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0\\le y<4,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(1+{y}^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(1-{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(1+{y}^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(1-{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}({f}_{X}(1+{y}^{1/2})+{f}_{X}(1-{y}^{1/2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}({f}_{X}(1+{y}^{1/2})+{f}_{X}(1-{y}^{1/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{1+{y}^{1/2}}{4}+\\frac{1-{y}^{1/2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{y}}, & 0\\le y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1034760-066d-5481-a644-ea198add71ec" :knowledge-point/title "One-to-One and Many-to-One Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109a4caa-a7e8-583f-9698-aaa055ed2e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551702} {:answer/id #uuid "214daf74-4940-5083-b578-63023e463520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551738} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b0364a-14f4-5f7b-a3b6-27cfa00e218d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552098} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd0856f-8424-5383-b2eb-ab6e741ffa8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552119} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f32439-073d-5d59-b043-3b15198e230f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "109a4caa-a7e8-583f-9698-aaa055ed2e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551702} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf9d5bd8-437e-59e4-b656-ae7d43e90ad8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97ae858a-243f-5feb-afeb-22f3b7dc533e" :question/math-academy-id "q-144034" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}, & 0<x<5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the probability density function of $Y=(X-3)^{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}g(y), & 0<y<4 \\\\ h(y), & 4\\le y<9 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the function $g(y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's visualize the transformation $Y=(X-3)^{2}$ over the interval $0<x<5.$\n\n![](images/6b/6b75d7be28fd4ec38257d1a8899960f38ce616e504e02f5f371620509afe81ac.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $0<x\\le 1.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $4\\le y<9.$\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $1<x<5.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $0<y<4.$\n\nIn this question, we're asked to consider only the interval $0<y<4.$\n\nUsing the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P((X-3)^{2}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-\\sqrt{y}\\le X-3\\le \\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =P(3-\\sqrt{y}\\le X\\le 3+\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(3+\\sqrt{y})-{F}_{X}(3-\\sqrt{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<4,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(3+\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(3-\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(3+\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(3-\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({f}_{X}(3+\\sqrt{y})+{f}_{X}(3-\\sqrt{y})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({f}_{X}(3+\\sqrt{y})+{f}_{X}(3-\\sqrt{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $g(y)=\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{y}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1034760-066d-5481-a644-ea198add71ec" :knowledge-point/title "One-to-One and Many-to-One Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2843be47-c7f9-5a97-a7a4-47db53522128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551756} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0083ba-2fd7-523d-87f0-800f414d2cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551926} {:answer/id #uuid "adb3f0ae-df16-5659-b325-3ceca4a652d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}(2y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552081} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ece98a-0d0c-598a-b2cd-f47cfde6d58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552138} {:answer/id #uuid "f04d15ee-484c-5036-8fec-b8c05f8c3ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(2y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f04d15ee-484c-5036-8fec-b8c05f8c3ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(2y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552227} :answer-field/id #uuid "005d2f41-cb9d-5db1-9f99-64e52b36e995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a04f88da-8653-5b9a-90b7-8404ed30a5a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-144270" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x, & 0<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the probability density function of $Y=\\frac{1}{2}|X-1|$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}g(y), & 0<y<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ h(y), & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the function $h(y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's visualize the transformation $Y=\\frac{1}{2}|X-1|$ over the interval $0<x<4.$\n\n![](images/16/163ffe5a4c5bcbce95369f8082c8dbfababe7c40315d8136d79c9e370f05a1a1.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $0<x<2.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $0<y<\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $2\\le x<4.$ On this interval,  $Y$ is nonzero on  $\\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nIn this question, we're asked to consider only the interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $\\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{1}{2}|X-1|\\le y) \\\\  & =P(|X-1|\\le 2y) \\\\  & =P(X-1\\le 2y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 2y+1) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(2y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used $|X-1|=X-1$ for $X>1.$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $\\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2},$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =2{F}_{X}^{′}(2y+1) \\\\  & =2{f}_{X}(2y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =2{f}_{X}(2y+1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}(2y+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $h(y)=\\frac{1}{4}(2y+1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176072 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f695215-606d-5c8b-87d2-dcf08bdc84e3" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Two-to-One Transformations to Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29aa6e8e-3373-5e59-b095-7eb1b3994fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{12}, & 1\\le y<5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551760} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6287b0-61c2-539d-9b03-9b32e898aa5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{y-1}, & 1\\le y<5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551770} {:answer/id #uuid "6b869697-82aa-50cd-8339-9f13dfa30a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{y}, & 0\\le y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551914} {:answer/id #uuid "dce0ee38-e2af-547b-830e-6d4ce2592e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}, & 0\\le y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552184} {:answer/id #uuid "f72e3ece-1a21-5772-b4d6-a7a44f7df06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{5}}, & 1\\le y<5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c6287b0-61c2-539d-9b03-9b32e898aa5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{y-1}, & 1\\le y<5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551770} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c65fe3f-7be0-53c6-b431-aa111757d6e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "738870fc-587d-5975-8073-552a681e8d2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-144343" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}{x}^{2}, & -2<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y={X}^{2}+1,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the transformation $Y={X}^{2}+1$ is two-to-one over the entire interval $-2<x<2.$ \n\n![](images/8a/8a03bd46aa6d415d8e1a49ff74557886702c1dc4bc9164ce2a5a1252874e1b4e.png)\n\nMoreover, for $-2<x<2,$  we have that $Y$ is nonzero on $1\\le y<5.$ \n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $1\\le y<5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P({X}^{2}+1\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-(y-1)^{1/2}\\le X\\le (y-1)^{1/2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}((y-1)^{1/2})-{F}_{X}(-(y-1)^{1/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $1\\le y<5,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}((y-1)^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(-(y-1)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {f}_{X}((y-1)^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(-(y-1)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}[{f}_{X}((y-1)^{1/2})+{f}_{X}(-(y-1)^{1/2})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}[{f}_{X}((y-1)^{1/2})+{f}_{X}(-(y-1)^{1/2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}[\\frac{3}{16}{((y-1)^{1/2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{16}{(-(y-1)^{1/2})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}(\\frac{3}{16}(y-1)+\\frac{3}{16}(y-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(y-1)^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{y-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{y-1}, & 1\\le y<5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1034760-066d-5481-a644-ea198add71ec" :knowledge-point/title "One-to-One and Many-to-One Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21119371-1c9a-57ce-9984-153f4f8d0228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551736} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba1eb2d-205f-5770-a63e-7397f1560cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551811} {:answer/id #uuid "43621ea0-b311-52e0-b829-6da60e2179fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551827} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc9d879-ed6b-5971-92aa-0c7e1f74c83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(2y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551890} {:answer/id #uuid "7b507010-23e5-5ac0-968d-faf96ad7a654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21119371-1c9a-57ce-9984-153f4f8d0228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551736} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e58226e-30cb-5bea-81aa-acfe6839c339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "858574ee-13d7-5994-a0b6-afd2faa00bae" :question/math-academy-id "q-144913" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x, & 0<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the probability density function of $Y=\\frac{1}{2}|X-1|$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}g(y), & 0<y<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ h(y), & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the function $g(y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's visualize the transformation $Y=\\frac{1}{2}|X-1|$ over the interval $0<x<4.$\n\n![](images/16/163ffe5a4c5bcbce95369f8082c8dbfababe7c40315d8136d79c9e370f05a1a1.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $0<x<2.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $0<y<\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $2\\le x<4.$ On this interval,  $Y$ is nonzero on  $\\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nIn this question, we're asked to consider only the interval $0<y<\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{1}{2}|X-1|\\le y) \\\\  & =P(|X-1|\\le 2y) \\\\  & =P(-2y\\le X-1\\le 2y) \\\\  & =P(1-2y\\le X\\le 2y+1) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(2y+1)-{F}_{X}(1-2y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<\\frac{1}{2},$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =2{F}_{X}^{′}(2y+1)+2{F}_{X}^{′}(1-2y) \\\\  & =2{f}_{X}(2y+1)+2{f}_{X}(1-2y) \\\\  & =2({f}_{X}(2y+1)+{f}_{X}(1-2y)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =2({f}_{X}(2y+1)+{f}_{X}(1-2y)) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{8}(2y+1)+\\frac{1}{8}(1-2y)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $g(y)=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1034760-066d-5481-a644-ea198add71ec" :knowledge-point/title "One-to-One and Many-to-One Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d6f1d0b-6f81-5cf4-bebf-3fad13b5e613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}-2}}{\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551815} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ae789e-901b-57ee-87ec-2d7f4a079fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}-2}+{e}^{\\sqrt{y}-2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552055} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e22714-2d28-5209-9c48-749b4258ee32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{y}-2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552069} {:answer/id #uuid "aa38616d-6833-526b-85db-50435de7fcc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}-2}+{e}^{\\sqrt{y}-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552072} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d27421-89f9-573b-bc7c-fff51999277b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}}+{e}^{\\sqrt{y}}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0ae789e-901b-57ee-87ec-2d7f4a079fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}-2}+{e}^{\\sqrt{y}-2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552055} :answer-field/id #uuid "03859fa3-6b6f-571d-948e-1aa4ee592774" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f55a1e43-e983-59db-880e-46aa1036ba76" :question/math-academy-id "q-144914" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2-x}, & x\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the probability density function of $Y=(X-4)^{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}g(y), & 0<y<4 \\\\ h(y), & y\\ge 4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the function $g(y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's visualize the transformation $Y=(X-4)^{2}$ over the interval $x\\ge 2.$\n\n![](images/77/77008820197d69dc81d674a48e207b7527d2f3d4cc1d0ca3ed8ad4d8553665b8.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $2<x<6.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $0<y<4.$\n\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $x\\ge 6.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $y\\ge 4.$\n\nIn this question, we're asked to consider only the interval $0<y<4.$\n\nUsing the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P((X-4)^{2}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-\\sqrt{y}\\le X-4\\le \\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =P(4-\\sqrt{y}\\le X\\le 4+\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(4+\\sqrt{y})-{F}_{X}(4-\\sqrt{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<4,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(4+\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(4-\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(4+\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(4-\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({f}_{X}(4+\\sqrt{y})+{f}_{X}(4-\\sqrt{y})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({f}_{X}(4+\\sqrt{y})+{f}_{X}(4-\\sqrt{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({e}^{2-4-\\sqrt{y}}+{e}^{2-4+\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}-2}+{e}^{\\sqrt{y}-2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $g(y)=\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}-2}+{e}^{\\sqrt{y}-2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1034760-066d-5481-a644-ea198add71ec" :knowledge-point/title "One-to-One and Many-to-One Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c9a6900-165c-50aa-95ea-de0c47286095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551726} {:answer/id #uuid "446def02-6674-5254-af04-09aaf48dacda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551833} {:answer/id #uuid "54918658-49fc-5a11-bdd9-d5c3dbb9bfcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}(2-\\sqrt{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551869} {:answer/id #uuid "8dda6381-ec5e-505b-bffc-e46959ca1981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{24\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552010} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a72f6f-818e-5187-8e3c-02bc8003c0e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c9a6900-165c-50aa-95ea-de0c47286095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551726} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dd61ae8-a6f5-545b-bade-944272de3e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a1ee509-91e0-56a1-9d72-7806568cd6ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-144920" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12}(2-x), & -3<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the probability density function of $Y={X}^{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}g(y), & 0<y<1 \\\\ h(y), & 1\\le y<9 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the function $g(y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's visualize the transformation $Y={X}^{2}$ over the interval $-3<x<1.$\n\n![](images/73/7332c58097d1a2933c10152028bc715a147e820ae14b2fae92fe288eaf956ce0.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $-3<x\\le -1.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $1\\le y<9.$\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $-1<x<1.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $0<y<1.$\n\nIn this question, we're asked to consider only the interval $0<y<1.$ \n\nUsing the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P({X}^{2}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-\\sqrt{y}\\le X\\le \\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(\\sqrt{y})-{F}_{X}(-\\sqrt{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(-\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(-\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({f}_{X}(\\sqrt{y})+{f}_{X}(-\\sqrt{y})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({f}_{X}(\\sqrt{y})+{f}_{X}(-\\sqrt{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}(\\frac{1}{12}(2-\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{12}(2+\\sqrt{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $g(y)=\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}}.$"}]}
